\chapter{Nominal impl blocks and associated members}
\label{ch:35}
\index{Nominal impl blocks and associated members}

An inherent \OnxCodeTerm{impl} adds associated functions, associated values, or eligible receiver members to a nominal record or enum. \OnxCodeTerm{Type::member} selects an associated member. \OnxCodeTerm{value::member} passes a copy-safe receiver as the first ordinary argument. Generic target identity and ordinary type inference decide the concrete function during compilation. The C11 output uses deterministic private functions.

\section{Check receiver semantics}
Use the core-impl example to compare a direct ordinary function with an equivalent value-receiver method. If a receiver expression computes a value, write a test for its evaluation count. Use an owner-bearing nominal type to exercise the negative boundary. The supported receiver profile is by value and copy-safe. Borrowing and moving receivers require additional rules, so express those operations with ordinary functions and explicit parameters in the supported ownership profile. Inherent lookup and intrinsic cliche resolution can share syntax while using different checked declaration rules.

\section{Read the marks before the sum}
The Counter example has two small functions that announce their evaluation. One prints 1 and returns a record whose field contains 40; the other prints 2 and returns the number 2. Predict the output of this line in isolation:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
println(marked_counter() :: add(marked_argument()));
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
First evaluate the expression on the left of the member selector. Its print supplies the first line and its resulting record supplies the first argument of the selected function. Next evaluate the explicit argument, producing the second line. Inside the member, read the field of the already computed receiver and add the extra argument. Only then does the enclosing print produce 42. Checking just the sum would miss both an accidental second evaluation of the receiver and an accidental reversal of the evaluation order.

The Pair example exercises a different part of lookup. Its implementation declares variables in the order \OnxCodeTerm{A, B} but targets \OnxCodeTerm{Pair<B,A>}. Match that head against the receiver \OnxCodeTerm{Pair<i64,bool>}: it binds the first target slot, hence B, to integer, and the second, hence A, to Boolean. The result annotation of \OnxCodeTerm{flip} is \OnxCodeTerm{(A,B)}. Applied to fields 8 and true it therefore returns true followed by 8. Matching the variables by declaration order would predict the wrong type as well as the wrong values. In both traces the receiver's nominal type selects the member; the contents of the record supply data for its body.

Notice also the final binding named Box in the example: after initialization that local shadows the type name. Its selector denotes a receiver operation even though the spelling starts with a capital letter.

